  "problem_addressed": "To provide an infrared- and collinear-safe inclusive jet clustering algorithm with regular, soft-resilient jet boundaries, comparable to idealised cone algorithms, for use in hadron-hadron collision analyses at the Tevatron and LHC.",
  "methodology": "The paper generalises sequential recombination jet algorithms by introducing a parameter p that controls the relative power of transverse momentum and rapidity-azimuth separation. For p = -1, the anti-kt algorithm uses d_ij = min(k_t_i^{-2}, k_t_j^{-2}) Delta_ij^2/R^2 and d_iB = k_t_i^{-2}, then repeatedly clusters the smallest distance or declares a jet. The authors analyse its boundary, area, non-global logarithm, Milan-factor and back-reaction properties, and test the algorithm using Monte Carlo samples and the FastJet implementation.",
  "experiments": "The authors compare kt, Cambridge/Aachen, SISCone and anti-kt by clustering a parton-level Herwig event plus random soft ghost particles, computing jet-area distributions and mean areas in LHC dijet samples generated with Pythia and Herwig, studying back-reaction after adding high-luminosity pileup, and performing a simple hadronic top-mass reconstruction in Pythia ttbar events.",
  "results": "Anti-kt hard jets are circular with radius R and are insensitive to soft radiation. Its passive and active areas are pi R^2, its area anomalous dimensions and area fluctuations are zero, and its area distribution is close to a delta function at pi R^2. Back-reaction is strongly suppressed compared with kt, Cambridge/Aachen and SISCone when high-luminosity pileup is added. In top-mass reconstruction the algorithms behave similarly, with Cambridge/Aachen and anti-kt performing marginally better than kt and SISCone.",
